ng)\n6. isAlive\n7. x, y (isAlive params)\n8. matches\n9. pattern\n10. x, y (every destructuring)\n11. input\n12. output\n\nTotal: 16\n\nConstants:\n1. `,` in template literal\n2. `[]` in `[...output]`\n3. `[]` in `return []`\n\nTotal: 3\n\nInvocations:\n1. new Set(...)\n2. cells.map(...)\n3. key(x, y) (inside map)\n4. liveCells.has(...)\n5. key(x, y) (inside has)\n6. pattern.every(...)\n7. isAlive(x, y) (inside every)\n8. matches(input)\n\nTotal: 8\n\nConditionals:\n1. `===` (size check)\n2. `&&` (short-circuit)\n3. `if` (in for body)\n\nTotal: 3\n\nLoops:\n1. for...of\n2. .map (implicit loop)\n3. .every (implicit loop)\n\nTotal: 3\n\nAssignments:\n- none\n\nTotal: 0\n\nMass = 3 + 16 + 16 + 12 + 15 + 0 = 62\n\nHmm, the exact numbers depend on interpretation. Let me just track the DELTA.\n\nAfter refactoring:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    pattern.length === liveCells.size &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)));\n\n  for (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nChanges:\n- Removed: `const isAlive = ...` line (3 bindings: isAlive, x, y)\n- Removed: `isAlive(x, y)` invocation\n- Added: `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` (2 invocations: has, key)\n\nBut wait, the old code had:\n- `isAlive(x, y)` inside `every` callback\n- which called `liveCells.has(key(x, y))`\n\nSo total invocations in original:\n- `isAlive(x, y)`: 1\n- Inside `isAlive`: `liveCells.has(...)`: 1\n- Inside `has`: `key(x, y)`: 1\n\nTotal per check: 3 invocations.\n\nIn refactored:\n- `liveCells.has(...)`: 1\n- `key(x, y)` inside: 1\n\nTotal per check: 2 invocations.\n\nSavings: 1 invocation per check.\n\nBut there's only 1 such check in the function (one call to `matches` which contains one `every` callback). So total savings: 1 invocation.\n\nNet change:\n- Bindings: -3 (isAlive, x, y for isAlive params)\n- Invocations: -1 (removed `isAlive(x, y)` call, but added `liveCells.has(...)` and `key(x, y)` which were previously nested)\n\nHmm, let me recount:\n\nBefore:\n- Invocation 1: `new Set(cells.map(...))`\n- Invocation 2: `cells.map(...)`\n- Invocation 3: `key(x, y)` inside map\n- Invocation 4: `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` (in isAlive body)\n- Invocation 5: `key(x, y)` inside has\n- Invocation 6: `pattern.every(...)` (in matches body)\n- Invocation 7: `isAlive(x, y)` (in every callback)\n- Invocation 8: `matches(input)` (in main loop)\n\nAfter:\n- Invocation 1: `new Set(cells.map(...))`\n- Invocation 2: `cells.map(...)`\n- Invocation 3: `key(x, y)` inside map\n- Invocation 4: `pattern.every(...)`\n- Invocation 5: `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` (in every callback)\n- Invocation 6: `key(x, y)` inside has\n- Invocation 7: `matches(input)`\n\nDifference: -1 invocation (removed the outer `isAlive` call, inlined the body which adds 2 calls but they're still 2 calls)\n\nWait no. Original had `isAlive(x, y)` = 1 call, which inside called `liveCells.has(...)` = 1 call, which inside called `key(x, y)` = 1 call. Total nested calls per match check: 3.\n\nRefactored has `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` = `liveCells.has(...)` = 1 call, plus `key(x, y)` = 1 call inside. Total: 2.\n\nSo refactored has 1 fewer invocation per check. Since there's 1 check per `every` callback which is called for each pattern cell, the savings depend on number of pattern cells. But for the algorithm structure, we save 1 invocation site.\n\nActually wait, the `every` callback is called many times. The 1 invocation site per check translates to N invocations across the loop. But we're counting structural elements, not dynamic invocations.\n\nOK, mass per code structure:\n- Before: 8 invocation sites\n- After: 7 invocation sites\n\nΔ: -1 invocation\n\nBindings:\n- Before: isAlive, x (isAlive), y (isAlive) = 3\n- After: 0\nΔ: -3\n\nConstants:\n- Before: 3\n- After: 3 (no change)\n\nConditionals:\n- Before: 3 (===, &&, if)\n- After: 3 (===, &&, if) - no change\n\nLoops:\n- Before: 3 (for-of, map, every)\n- After: 3 (for-of, map, every) - no change\n\nAssignments:\n- Before: 0\n- After: 0 - no change\n\nMass delta:\n- Bindings: -3 * 1 = -3\n- Invocations: -1 * 2 = -2\n- Total: -5\n\nOK so mass goes down by 5. That's a modest improvement.\n\nNow let me also reconsider the `pattern.length === liveCells.size` reordering. This doesn't change mass (same operators, same operands), but might improve readability (Rule 2).\n\nReading the current code: \"live cells size equals pattern length\" — actual first, expected second.\nAfter reordering: \"pattern length equals live cells size\" — expected first, actual second.\n\nHmm, both are valid English. I'll keep the new order because \"pattern.length\" (the thing we're matching against) comes first, which feels more natural. Actually wait, in the current code, `liveCells` (the thing we're querying) comes first. Both make sense.\n\nYou know what, let me not bother with the reorder. It's a wash for mass and a wash for readability.\n\nActually, I'll do the reorder because I want to make the diff as minimal as possible while still making an improvement. The reorder makes it clearer that we're checking \"does the pattern size match the live cells size?\" — putting the pattern (the thing being compared) first.\n\nWait, looking at this more carefully:\n\n`liveCells.size === pattern.length`\n- Subject: `liveCells.size` (the actual count)\n- Verb: `===`\n- Object: `pattern.length` (the expected count)\n\n`pattern.length === liveCells.size`\n- Subject: `pattern.length` (the expected count)\n- Verb: `===`\n- Object: `liveCells.size` (the actual count)\n\nIn English, we'd typically say \"the expected matches the actual\", which is `expected === actual`. So `pattern.length === liveCells.size` is more natural.\n\nOK, reorder it is.\n\nAlright, applying the refactoring now.\n\nLet me write the diff:\n\nOLD:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  const isAlive = (x: number, y: number) => liveCells.has(key(x, y));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    liveCells.size === pattern.length &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => isAlive(x, y));\n\n  for (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nNEW:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    pattern.length === liveCells.size &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)));\n\n  for (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nChanges:\n1. Removed line `const isAlive = (x: number, y: number) => liveCells.has(key(x, y));`\n2. Reordered `liveCells.size === pattern.length` to `pattern.length === liveCells.size`\n3. Changed `pattern.every(([x, y]) => isAlive(x, y))` to `pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)))`\n\nLet me apply this with the edit tool.\n\nActually, before I do, let me think if there's a bigger refactoring I'm missing.\n\nLooking at PATTERNS:\n```typescript\nconst PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[] = [\n  // ...5 entries\n];\n```\n\nThis is data. Each entry has an input and output. The structure is clear.\n\nCould PATTERNS be cleaner? Maybe a Map? Or indexed by hash? Probably not — the linear search is fine for 5 entries, and a Map doesn't simplify.\n\nCould the comments be moved into a separate documentation? They're fine as inline comments.\n\nOK, I think the refactoring is solid. Let me apply it.\n\nOne more thought: the `readonly` keyword on PATTERNS. Is that necessary? Yes, because we don't want callers to mutate the lookup table. Keep it.\n\nLet me apply the refactoring.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"I have the full picture. Let me apply the systematic refactoring process.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n- `nextGeneration` — clearly describes what it does\n- `PATTERNS` — clear, with explanatory comment\n- `key` — clear local helper for canonical cell-to-string conversion (used twice → DRY value)\n- `liveCells` — preserves the abstraction \"set of live cells\" rather than exposing the string-key implementation\n- `matches` — clear, with comment\n- **`isAlive`** — used at exactly one call site, wraps `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` which is already the canonical \"is alive\" check. **Redundant single-use abstraction.**\n\n## Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\n\n```typescript\nconst key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\nconst liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\nconst isAlive = (x: number, y: number) => liveCells.has(key(x, y));\nconst matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n  liveCells.size === pattern.length &&\n  pattern.every(([x, y]) => isAlive(x, y));\n\nfor (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n  if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n}\nreturn [];\n```\n\n**Component Count:**\n- Constants: `,`, `[]` (spread), `[]` (empty return) = 3\n- Bindings: cells, key, x, y (key), liveCells, x, y (map), isAlive, x, y (isAlive), matches, pattern, x, y (every), input, output = 16\n- Invocations: new Set, cells.map, key (in map), liveCells.has, key (in has), pattern.every, isAlive, matches = 8\n- Conditionals: ===, &&, if = 3\n- Loops: for-of, map, every = 3\n- Assignments: 0\n\n**Initial Mass**: 3·1 + 16·1 + 8·2 + 3·4 + 3·5 + 0·6 = 3 + 16 + 16 + 12 + 15 + 0 = **62**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n### Rule 1: Tests Pass\nConfirmed: 8 tests passing.\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\nTwo issues:\n1. `isAlive` indirection adds a layer without clarifying `liveCells.has(key(x, y))` — the underlying expression IS the definition of \"is alive\".\n2. `liveCells.size === pattern.length` reads \"actual equals expected\". Reversing to `pattern.length === liveCells.size` reads naturally as \"expected equals actual\", aligning with how we describe pattern matching in English.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\nNo meaningful duplication; `key` is used twice (legitimate DRY).\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n`isAlive` is a single-use helper that wraps a self-expressing expression. Remove it.\n\n## Step 4: Implement Refactoring"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_460416104d0242d3a680fd72","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    pattern.length === liveCells.size &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)));","oldText":"  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  const isAlive = (x: number, y: number) => liveCells.has(key(x, y));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    liveCells.size === pattern.length &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => isAlive(x, y));"}],"path":"/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-07-25_09-28-53_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.2.1-phase-continuation-pi_minimax-m3/src/game-of-life.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/minimax-m3","usage":{"input":335,"output":13680,"cacheRead":9472,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":12689,"totalTokens":23487,"cost":{"input":0,"output"