s odd. Let me verify by checking the actual cells:\n\nPattern 5 input: [1,0], [0,1], [2,1], [1,2]\nPattern 5 output: [0,1], [1,0], [1,2], [2,1]\n\nCells in input: { (1,0), (0,1), (2,1), (1,2) }\nCells in output: { (0,1), (1,0), (1,2), (2,1) }\n\nSame set of cells! Order doesn't matter for the algorithm (uses Set). But it's inconsistent in the source.\n\nOK so my refactoring would naturally unify the order.\n\nAlright, let me also consider: should I sort the cells in DIAMOND for canonical ordering? Like:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [2, 1]]; // sorted by y then x\n```\n\nOr keep the natural shape ordering:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]]; // top, left, right, bottom\n```\n\nThe second is more descriptive of the diamond shape. Let me use that.\n\nOK here's my refactoring:\n\n```typescript\n/** A live cell at integer grid coordinate [x, y]. */\nexport type Cell = [number, number];\n\ntype Pattern = { input: Cell[]; output: Cell[] };\n\n// Diamond shape: 4 cells in a diamond around an empty center.\n// Used as both a still life and as the output of the plus-sign transition.\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n\n// Each row pairs an input pattern with the cells alive after one generation.\n// Adding a new pattern means appending one row -- no other code changes.\nconst PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[] = [\n  // Rule 2 (lower bound): 3-in-a-row -> middle (1,0) survives, ends die.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]], output: [[1, 0]] },\n  // Rule 2 (upper bound): L-tetromino -> L-tromino with (1,0) surviving.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]] },\n  // Rule 4 Reproduction: L-tromino -> (1,1) joins as the new live cell.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]] },\n  // Beehive still life: corner dead cells have 2 live neighbors and stay dead.\n  {\n    input: [[1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1], [3, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]],\n    output: [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [2, 0], [2, 2], [3, 1]],\n  },\n  // Diamond still life: dead center (1,1) has 4 live neighbors and stays\n  // dead (Rule 4 requires exactly 3). 4 corner cells survive with 2 each.\n  { input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n  // Rule 3 (lower bound): plus sign -> center (1,1) has 4 neighbors and dies.\n  // 4 arm cells each have 3 neighbors -> survive. Result: diamond.\n  { input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n];\n```\n\nWait, but `input: [...DIAMOND]` requires DIAMOND to be spread. The spread makes a new array. Then the pattern is `{ input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] }` which is two different arrays with the same content. That's correct.\n\nHmm but then `input: DIAMOND` (without spread) would reference the same array as `output: DIAMOND`. That's OK because we don't mutate.\n\nActually, looking at the patterns in the original code:\n```\n{ input: [[1, 0], [2, 0], ...], output: [[0, 1], [1, 0], ...] }\n```\n\nEach input and output is a fresh array literal. To preserve this style, I should use `[...DIAMOND]` for each reference.\n\nOr I can use `DIAMOND` directly and rely on the immutability. Since the function does `return [...output]`, it creates a new array anyway, so the caller never gets a reference to DIAMOND.\n\nLet me use the spread to be safe and to match the existing style of fresh array literals.\n\nActually, I think using DIAMOND directly is cleaner:\n```typescript\n{ input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n```\n\nvs.\n```typescript\n{ input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n```\n\nThe first is shorter. The risk is if someone later mutates `input` or `output`, it would mutate DIAMOND. But that's already a risk in the current code (anywhere we return a literal, someone could write code to mutate it).\n\nHmm, let me think about what the original code does:\n```typescript\nreturn [...output];\n```\n\nThis creates a copy. So the caller never gets a reference to the PATTERNS array's elements. Good.\n\nBut within the PATTERNS structure itself, if `input: DIAMOND` and `output: DIAMOND`, both point to the same array. The `matches` function does:\n```typescript\npattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)))\n```\n\nThis reads cells but doesn't mutate. So it's safe.\n\nOK I'll use DIAMOND directly for clarity:\n```typescript\n{ input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n```\n\nFor pattern 6, the input needs to be DIAMOND plus [1,1]. I can use spread:\n```typescript\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n```\n\nHmm, but this creates a new array. Consistent with the original style.\n\nOK let me also think about: should I update the still life comment to be more informative?\n\nThe diamond still life comment says:\n```\n// Diamond still life: dead center (1,1) has 4 live neighbors and stays\n// dead (Rule 4 requires exactly 3). 4 corner cells survive with 2 each.\n```\n\nThis is correct but verbose. The 4 \"corner\" cells each have 2 live neighbors? Let me verify:\n- (1,0): neighbors (0,0), (2,0), (0,1), (1,1), (2,1). Live: (0,1), (2,1). That's 2. ✓\n- (0,1): neighbors (0,0), (1,0), (-1,1), (0,2), (1,1), (1,2). Live: (1,0), (1,2). That's 2. ✓\n- (2,1): similarly 2. ✓\n- (1,2): similarly 2. ✓\n\nAnd dead center (1,1) has 4 live neighbors: (1,0), (0,1), (2,1), (1,2). Doesn't reproduce (needs exactly 3). ✓\n\nSo the comment is correct. I'll keep it.\n\nAlright, let me commit to this refactoring and run the tests.\n\nBut wait, I also want to think about whether to extract BEEHIVE similarly. The beehive is only used in one pattern entry (as a still life), so extracting wouldn't reduce duplication. But for consistency, maybe both should be extracted as named shapes?\n\nHmm. Let me think about this. If I extract DIAMOND but not BEEHIVE, the code is inconsistent. If I extract both, the beehive extraction is just for naming consistency.\n\nThe argument for extracting both: makes the code more readable (you can see at a glance what shapes are used).\nThe argument against: adds 1 extra binding for the beehive, only minor benefit.\n\nHmm. Let me extract just DIAMOND for now, and document that I considered BEEHIVE but decided not to extract it.\n\nActually, you know what, let me look at this one more time. The point of the refactoring exercise is to improve code quality. Let me make sure my refactoring actually improves quality.\n\nPRO extraction:\n- Names a repeated shape, making the relationship between patterns 5 and 6 explicit\n- Eliminates redundant cell literals\n- Shows the \"diamond is a still life and is also a pattern output\" relationship\n\nCON extraction:\n- Adds one more binding\n- Slightly different style from other patterns (which use inline literals)\n\nI think PRO wins. The relationship between patterns 5 and 6 (diamond survives both as itself and as the output of plus sign) is meaningful and worth expressing.\n\nLet me also check: are there any tests that depend on the input array being a specific instance? Looking at the test file... no, the tests use sortCells which creates new arrays. And they only check cell coordinates.\n\nOK, here's my refactoring plan:\n\n1. Add `DIAMOND` constant at module level\n2. Update pattern 5 to use DIAMOND for both input and output\n3. Update pattern 6 to use `[...DIAMOND, [1, 1]]` for input and DIAMOND for output\n4. Add a brief comment explaining what DIAMOND represents\n5. Run tests\n6. Calculate APP mass before/after\n7. Document the refactoring\n\nLet me also think: should the plus sign pattern's input be expressed differently? Currently: `[...DIAMOND, [1, 1]]`. This means \"diamond plus center cell (1,1)\". Conceptually, \"the diamond plus its center is a plus sign\". So `[...DIAMOND, [1, 1]]` is the plus sign.\n\nAlternative: define PLUS_SIGN = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]] and reference it. But this doesn't show the relationship to DIAMOND.\n\nI think `[...DIAMOND, [1, 1]]` is more informative.\n\nAlright, let me write the refactored code:\n\n```typescript\n/** A live cell at integer grid coordinate [x, y]. */\nexport type Cell = [number, number];\n\ntype Pattern = { input: Cell[]; output: Cell[] };\n\n// Diamond shape: 4 cells in a diamond around an empty center (1,1).\n// Reused as a still life and as the output of the plus-sign transition.\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n\n// Each row pairs an input pattern with the cells alive after one generation.\n// Adding a new pattern means appending one row -- no other code changes.\nconst PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[] = [\n  // Rule 2 (lower bound): 3-in-a-row -> middle (1,0) survives, ends die.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]], output: [[1, 0]] },\n  // Rule 2 (upper bound): L-tetromino -> L-tromino with (1,0) surviving.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]] },\n  // Rule 4 Reproduction: L-tromino -> (1,1) joins as the new live cell.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]] },\n  // Beehive still life: corner dead cells have 2 live neighbors and stay dead.\n  {\n    input: [[1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1], [3, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]],\n    output: [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [2, 0], [2, 2], [3, 1]],\n  },\n  // Diamond still life: dead center (1,1) has 4 live neighbors and stays\n  // dead (Rule 4 requires exactly 3). 4 corner cells survive with 2 each.\n  { input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n  // Rule 3 (lower bound): plus sign -> center (1,1) has 4 neighbors and dies.\n  // 4 arm cells each have 3 neighbors -> survive. Result: diamond.\n  { input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n];\n```\n\nWait, but pattern 5's input and output reference the SAME array (DIAMOND). The `matches` function will read from it, which is fine. But there's a potential confusion: if someone modifies the array later, it would affect both. Since `DIAMOND` is `readonly`, the type system prevents accidental mutation. \n\nActually, `DIAMOND: readonly Cell[]` means it can't be mutated. So `input: DIAMOND` is safe.\n\nBut then `input: Cell[]` is the type, not `readonly Cell[]`. So the assignment from DIAMOND to input loses the readonly. That's fine because input is just an interface, not a stored property.\n\nWait, let me check the Pattern type: `type Pattern = { input: Cell[]; output: Cell[] };`\n\nYes, input and output are mutable arrays. But since we never mutate them (just read), it's safe.\n\nOK the refactoring is solid. Let me implement it.\n\nActually, one more consideration: type safety. `DIAMOND` is `readonly Cell[]`. Assigning to `input: Cell[]` should work (covariant assignment). Let me double-check.\n\n`readonly Cell[]` is assignable to `Cell[]`? Hmm, actually I think it might be the other way around: `Cell[]` is assignable to `readonly Cell[]` (you can treat mutable as readonly), but NOT the reverse.\n\nLet me think... In TypeScript, `readonly T[]` is a supertype of `T[]`. So `T[]` is assignable to `readonly T[]` but not vice versa.\n\nSo `DIAMOND: readonly Cell[]` cannot be directly assigned to `input: Cell[]`. We need `[...DIAMOND]` or `Array.from(DIAMOND)` to make a mutable copy.\n\nHmm, that's a wrinkle. Let me reconsider.\n\nOptions:\n1. Make DIAMOND mutable: `const DIAMOND: Cell[] = [...];`\n2. Spread everywhere: `input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND]`\n3. Make Pattern.input/output readonly\n\nOption 1 is the simplest. DIAMOND is not mutated anywhere, so making it mutable doesn't cause bugs. We just lose the safety guarantee.\n\nOption 2 is safest but adds noise.\n\nOption 3 would propagate readonly, which might be too restrictive if we ever want to mutate.\n\nLet me go with Option 1 for simplicity:\n\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n```\n\nWait, `const DIAMOND: Cell[]` means the binding is const but the array contents can be mutated. That's the same as the existing inline arrays. So no extra risk.\n\nBut wait, the issue is: `const PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[]` and `type Pattern = { input: Cell[]; output: Cell[]; }`. If `input` and `output` are `Cell[]`, then `readonly Pattern[]` means we can't reassign pattern entries but their arrays are still mutable.\n\nOK let me just go with `const DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];` and use it directly.\n\nOr I can be explicit with spread:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]] as const;\n// but `as const` makes it readonly tuple...\n```\n\nHmm, `as const` would make it `readonly [readonly [1, 0], readonly [0, 1], ...]` which is too restrictive.\n\nLet me just use `const DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];` and spread when assigning:\n\n```typescript\n{ input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n```\n\nThis makes the readonly explicit (no one can accidentally mutate DIAMOND), and the spread creates fresh arrays for each pattern entry.\n\nAlternatively, just use mutable:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n```\n\nAnd reference directly:\n```typescript\n{ input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n```\n\nThis is cleaner but DIAMOND could be mutated.\n\nHmm. Let me think about which is safer. The function does:\n```typescript\nfor (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n}\n```\n\n`matches(input)` reads input but doesn't mutate. `return [...output]` reads output and creates a copy. No mutation.\n\nSo in this code, DIAMOND is never mutated. Making it readonly is a defensive measure.\n\nOK let me go with:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n```\n\nAnd use it with spread:\n```typescript\n{ input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n```\n\nHmm but this is more verbose. Let me check if I can use DIAMOND directly with readonly Pattern.\n\nActually, I can change Pattern to have readonly fields:\n```typescript\ntype Pattern = { input: readonly Cell[]; output: readonly Cell[] };\n```\n\nAnd PATTERNS becomes:\n```typescript\nconst PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[] = [...];\n```\n\nThen `input: DIAMOND` works because DIAMOND is readonly Cell[].\n\nBut this changes the existing Pattern type. Does anything else depend on Cell[] mutability? Let me check the test file... No, tests just sort and compare.\n\nHmm, but changing Pattern type is more invasive. Let me just spread.\n\nOK final decision:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND: readonly Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n```\n\nAnd in PATTERNS:\n```typescript\n{ input: [...DIAMOND], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: [...DIAMOND] },\n```\n\nThis is defensive (readonly) but explicit (spread each time).\n\nWait, I keep going back and forth. Let me just pick one and commit.\n\nOK using:\n```typescript\nconst DIAMOND = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]] as Cell[];\n\n// Or just\nconst DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n```\n\nAnd:\n```typescript\n{ input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n{ input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n```\n\nWhy mutable? Because:\n1. PATTERN's input/output are already mutable (Cell[])\n2. We never mutate them anyway\n3. Simpler syntax\n\nOK committing to this.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about whether to extract other shapes for symmetry. Looking at patterns:\n1. 3-in-a-row: appears once\n2. L-tetromino: appears once\n3. L-tromino: appears once (input) and once as output of pattern 2\n4. Beehive: appears once\n5. Diamond: appears twice (pattern 5 and as output of pattern 6)\n6. Plus sign: appears once (input), output is diamond\n\nThe L-tromino also has a relationship: it's the output of pattern 2 AND the input of pattern 3. So there's a chain: L-tetromino -> L-tromino -> L-tromino+1.\n\nCould we extract L_TROMINO too?\n\nPattern 2 output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]]\nPattern 3 input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]]\n\nSame cells, different order.\n\nYes! L-tromino also appears in two pattern entries. Should I extract it too?\n\nLet me think... Yes, this would be consistent. If I extract DIAMOND, I should also extract L_TROMINO.\n\n```typescript\nconst L_TROMINO: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]];\nconst DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n\nconst PATTERNS = [\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]], output: [[1, 0]] },\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [...L_TROMINO] },\n  { input: [...L_TROMINO], output: [...L_TROMINO, [1, 1]] },\n  // beehive\n  { input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n  { input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n];\n```\n\nThis is more meaningful! Now the chains are:\n- L-tetromino -> L-tromino (pattern 2)\n- L-tromino -> L-tromino + (1,1) (pattern 3, reproduction)\n- Diamond -> Diamond (pattern 5, still life)\n- Plus sign -> Diamond (pattern 6)\n\nThis makes the relationships explicit. \n\nBut wait, the patterns also have their own names (like \"3-in-a-row\", \"L-tetromino\", \"L-tromino\"). Maybe I should extract those too?\n\nPattern 1: 3-in-a-row (input) -> middle (output)\nPattern 2: L-tetromino (input) -> L-tromino (output)\n\nThe \"3-in-a-row\" input is just `[[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]]` which is similar to the L-tetromino minus the bottom-left cell. They're not exactly the same cells but they share the horizontal row of 3.\n\nHmm, extracting too many shapes could clutter the code. Let me limit to L_TROMINO and DIAMOND, which are both outputs of one pattern AND inputs/related-to another.\n\nActually, the 3-in-a-row only appears in pattern 1, so no benefit to extracting. The L-tetromino only appears in pattern 2 as input. The L-tromino appears in pattern 2 as output AND pattern 3 as input. The diamond appears in pattern 5 as input AND pattern 6 as output.\n\nSo extracting L_TROMINO and DIAMOND is justified.\n\nOK my refactoring plan:\n1. Extract L_TROMINO and DIAMOND as named constants\n2. Use them in the appropriate pattern entries\n3. Update comments if needed\n4. Run tests\n5. Calculate APP mass\n6. Document\n\nHmm wait, but extracting more constants increases mass (more bindings). Let me think about whether this is a net win.\n\nOriginal:\n```typescript\n{ input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]] },\n{ input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]], output: [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]] },\n```\n\nvs refactored:\n```typescript\nconst L_TROMINO: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]];\n// ...\n{ input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [...L_TROMINO] },\n{ input: [...L_TROMINO], output: [...L_TROMINO, [1, 1]] },\n```\n\nCell counts:\n- Original: 3 + 3 = 6 cells in pattern 2 output and pattern 3 input\n- Refactored: 3 cells in L_TROMINO, then 0 (replaced by spread) = 3 cells total\n\nSo we saved 3 cell literals. Plus added 1 binding (L_TROMINO).\n\nMass change:\n- Removed: 6 cell literals (constants) = 6\n- Added: 1 binding (L_TROMINO) = 1, 2 spread invocations = 4\n- Net: -1 mass (slight reduction)\n\nHmm, marginal. The real benefit is conceptual clarity.\n\nOK let me also think: should I bother extracting L_TROMINO? The relationship is:\n- Pattern 2: L-tetromino input -> L-tromino output\n- Pattern 3: L-tromino input -> L-tromino + (1,1) output\n\nThe fact that the output of pattern 2 is the input of pattern 3 is meaningful - it shows that the L-tromino is a \"fixed point\" of pattern 3 (it becomes itself plus one new cell).\n\nExtracting L_TROMINO makes this relationship explicit.\n\nOK I'll do both extractions.\n\nActually wait, one more consideration. Let me re-read the prompt:\n\n\"Do NOT generalize beyond what current tests demand.\"\n\nExtracting constants isn't generalizing. It's refactoring. It's making existing knowledge more explicit. So this should be fine.\n\nAlright, let me commit and implement.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time. The prompt says:\n\"Refactor while keeping all 9 tests green.\"\n\nAnd:\n\"Do NOT generalize beyond what current tests demand.\"\n\nThese suggest: don't add features, don't change behavior. Just improve code quality.\n\nMy refactoring:\n- Extract DIAMOND and L_TROMINO as named constants\n- Use them in pattern entries\n\nThis:\n- Doesn't change behavior (tests still pass)\n- Doesn't generalize (no new patterns, no new logic)\n- Improves code quality (named constants, less duplication)\n\nOK this fits.\n\nLet me implement now.\n\nWait, I also want to think about whether to handle the beehive differently. The beehive appears only once (as input and output of pattern 4). The input and output have different orderings:\n\nInput: [[1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1], [3, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]]\nOutput: [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [2, 0], [2, 2], [3, 1]]\n\nThe cells are the same. Should I extract BEEHIVE and use it for both input and output?\n\n```typescript\nconst BEEHIVE: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1], [3, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]];\n{ input: BEEHIVE, output: BEEHIVE },\n```\n\nThis would be consistent with the diamond extraction. The beehive is also a still life (input == output).\n\nHmm, but beehive is only in one pattern entry, so extracting doesn't reduce duplication. It only makes the still-life concept more explicit.\n\nIf I extract L_TROMINO and DIAMOND (both used in multiple entries), I should also extract BEEHIVE for consistency.\n\nBut wait, BEEHIVE extraction has no real benefit except consistency. Let me consider:\n\nFor consistency:\n- DIAMOND is used in 2 entries, extracted (saves duplication)\n- L_TROMINO is used in 2 entries, extracted (saves duplication)\n- BEEHIVE is used in 1 entry, NOT extracted (would be for consistency only)\n\nInconsistent. To make it consistent, either:\na) Extract BEEHIVE for consistency (but no duplication benefit)\nb) Inline BEEHIVE's cells (no extraction)\nc) Extract BEEHIVE only if it's actually a still life that we want to name\n\nHmm. Let me look at this from a different angle: what