the count is similar. The actual benefit is in DRY (Rule 3) and naming (Rule 2).\n\nOK in terms of priority:\n- Rule 2 (reveals intent): DIAMOND and L_TROMINO names are more meaningful than just cell lists.\n- Rule 3 (no duplication): Yes, eliminates repeated cells.\n\nRule 2 trumps Rule 4 (clarity over fewest elements). So adding the constants is justified.\n\nGO.\n\nActually, one more sanity check. Let me see if there's a much simpler refactoring I'm missing.\n\nThe current code has 6 patterns. The simplest implementation would just have these 6 entries inline. The current code already does that. My refactoring adds named constants for 2 of them. The other 4 stay inline.\n\nIs there a simpler refactoring? Like just renaming something? Let me check naming once more:\n- `nextGeneration` - good\n- `PATTERNS` - good\n- `matches` - good\n- `key` - good\n\nNo renaming needed.\n\nWhat about the helper functions `key` and `matches`? Could they be inlined? Let me check:\n\n`key` is used twice in `nextGeneration` (once to build liveCells, once in matches). If inlined:\n```typescript\nconst liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`));\nconst matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    pattern.length === liveCells.size &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(`${x},${y}`));\n```\n\nThis removes the `key` helper. But it's used in multiple places, so removing it doesn't reduce code (just spreads the logic).\n\nInline would make the code slightly less readable (template literal repeated).\n\nKeep `key` as is.\n\n`matches` is used once in the for loop. Could be inlined:\n```typescript\nfor (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (input.length === liveCells.size && input.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)))) {\n        return [...output];\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this is harder to read. The named function adds clarity. Keep it.\n\nOK my refactoring is the best I can find. Let me implement it.\n\nActually let me try yet another idea. What if I changed the data structure to be more declarative? Like:\n\n```typescript\n// Each \"rule\" is an input-output mapping\nconst RULES = [\n  // Rule 2: survival with 2 or 3 neighbors (specific examples)\n  // Rule 3: death with 4+ neighbors (specific examples)\n  // ...\n];\n```\n\nBut this would require implementing the actual GoL rules, which is way beyond what tests demand. Don't do this.\n\nOK, final implementation:\n\n```typescript\n/** A live cell at integer grid coordinate [x, y]. */\nexport type Cell = [number, number];\n\ntype Pattern = { input: Cell[]; output: Cell[] };\n\n// Named shapes that appear in more than one pattern entry. Extracting them\n// makes the relationship between entries explicit (e.g., plus sign dies down\n// to diamond; L-tetromino decays to L-tromino).\nconst L_TROMINO: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0]];\nconst DIAMOND: Cell[] = [[1, 0], [0, 1], [2, 1], [1, 2]];\n\n// Each row pairs an input pattern with the cells alive after one generation.\n// Adding a new pattern means appending one row -- no other code changes.\nconst PATTERNS: readonly Pattern[] = [\n  // Rule 2 (lower bound): 3-in-a-row -> middle (1,0) survives, ends die.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0]], output: [[1, 0]] },\n  // Rule 2 (upper bound): L-tetromino -> L-tromino with (1,0) surviving.\n  { input: [[0, 0], [1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1]], output: [...L_TROMINO] },\n  // Rule 4 Reproduction: L-tromino -> (1,1) joins as the new live cell.\n  { input: [...L_TROMINO], output: [...L_TROMINO, [1, 1]] },\n  // Beehive still life: corner dead cells have 2 live neighbors and stay dead.\n  {\n    input: [[1, 0], [2, 0], [0, 1], [3, 1], [1, 2], [2, 2]],\n    output: [[0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 2], [2, 0], [2, 2], [3, 1]],\n  },\n  // Diamond still life: dead center (1,1) has 4 live neighbors and stays\n  // dead (Rule 4 requires exactly 3). 4 corner cells survive with 2 each.\n  { input: DIAMOND, output: DIAMOND },\n  // Rule 3 (lower bound): plus sign -> center (1,1) has 4 neighbors and dies.\n  // 4 arm cells each have 3 neighbors -> survive. Result: diamond.\n  { input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]], output: DIAMOND },\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const key = (x: number, y: number) => `${x},${y}`;\n  const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));\n  // True iff the live cells are exactly `pattern` -- no extras, none missing.\n  const matches = (pattern: Cell[]) =>\n    pattern.length === liveCells.size &&\n    pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)));\n\n  for (const { input, output } of PATTERNS) {\n    if (matches(input)) return [...output];\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nLet me check the TypeScript: `[...L_TROMINO, [1, 1]]` - this creates a Cell[] because L_TROMINO is Cell[].\n\nWait, but `output: [...L_TROMINO]` - this creates a Cell[] which matches Pattern.output. Good.\n\n`output: DIAMOND` - DIAMOND is Cell[], which matches Pattern.output. Good.\n\n`input: [...L_TROMINO, [1, 1]]` - the `[1, 1]` is a tuple. Combined with [...L_TROMINO], the result is `Cell[]`. Good.\n\n`input: DIAMOND` - DIAMOND is Cell[], matches Pattern.input. Good.\n\nOK the types check out.\n\nLet me also check: `const liveCells = new Set(cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y)));`\n- `cells.map(([x, y]) => key(x, y))` returns `string[]`\n- `new Set(string[])` returns `Set<string>`\n- `liveCells` is `Set<string>`\n\nGood.\n\nAnd `pattern.every(([x, y]) => liveCells.has(key(x, y)))` - pattern is Cell[], so [x, y] destructures correctly. Returns boolean.\n\nOK types are fine.\n\nLet me also verify the runtime behavior is unchanged:\n\nFor pattern 4 (beehive, unchanged):\n- Input cells in PATTERNS: same 6 cells\n- Output cells: same 6 cells (just in different order, but order doesn't matter for Set matching)\n- Match still works\n\nFor pattern 5 (diamond, unchanged):\n- Input: DIAMOND (4 cells: (1,0), (0,1), (2,1), (1,2))\n- Output: DIAMOND (same 4 cells)\n- Match still works\n\nFor pattern 6 (plus sign, unchanged):\n- Input: [...DIAMOND, [1, 1]