: Tests Pass ✓ (just verified)\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\nThe named constants `L_TROMINO`, `DIAMOND`, `BLOCK` make relationships between patterns explicit. The Blinker entries (9 & 10) duplicate the same cell arrays inline — vertical and horizontal forms cycle into each other, but this relationship is only in comments, not in the code. Extracting `BLINKER_V` and `BLINKER_H` would make the cycle visible at the code level.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n**Duplication found**: The two Blinker entries contain literal duplicates of each other:\n- Entry 9 output `[[-1,1],[0,1],[1,1]]` == Entry 10 input `[[-1,1],[0,1],[1,1]]`\n- Entry 9 input `[[0,0],[0,1],[0,2]]` == Entry 10 output `[[0,0],[0,1],[0,2]]`\n\nThis violates the existing convention where `L_TROMINO`, `DIAMOND`, `BLOCK` are extracted. The Blinker shapes are also used twice — perfect candidates for named constants following the existing pattern.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\nAdding two constants is minimal. The alternative (using nested constant references inside PATTERNS without extraction) would only save mass, not improve clarity.\n\n## Refactoring Decision\n\nExtract `BLINKER_V` and `BLINKER_H` constants following the existing convention, and update the top comment to document the new relationship.\n\nThis is a clear win:\n- Rule 2: Reveals the V↔H cycle in code, not just comments\n- Rule 3: Removes literal duplication\n- Rule 4: Minimal additions (2 constants)\n- Doesn't generalize beyond what tests demand — uses existing test-defined data","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Tests are green. Now let me analyze the implementation.\n\n## Step 1: Naming Evaluation\n\n**Current names analysis:**\n- `nextGeneration` — clear, reveals intent ✓\n- `cellKey` — clear helper name for converting Cell to Set key ✓\n- `liveCells` — slight imprecision (Set of string keys, not Cells), but the comment clarifies ✓\n- `matches` — clear comparator name ✓\n- `expected` (param in `matches`) — reads naturally in context ✓\n- `L_TROMINO`, `DIAMOND`, `BLOCK` — excellent named shapes ✓\n- `PATTERNS` — clear ✓\n\n## Step 2: APP Mass Calculation (Current)\n\nMatcher logic in `nextGeneration`:\n- Constants: 1 (the `[]` return)\n- Bindings: cells, cellKey, x, y, liveCells, matches, expected, input, output ≈ 9\n- Invocations: template literal, new Set, map, cellKey×2, length, size, every, has, matches ≈ 10\n- Loops: map, every, for-of = 3\n- Conditionals: if = 1\n- Assignments: 0\n\nMass ≈ 1 + 9 + 20 + 15 + 4 = **49**\n\n## Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules\n\n**Rule 1 (Tests Pass)**: ✓ 13 passing.\n\n**Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: The existing constants `L_TROMINO`, `DIAMOND`, `BLOCK` are extracted precisely because the same shapes appear in multiple entries — making the relationships between patterns explicit. The Blinker entries (9 & 10) violate this convention: the same vertical and horizontal cell arrays appear inline, and the cycle between them is documented only in comments.\n\n**Rule 3 (No Duplication)**: Clear duplication found:\n- Entry 9 output `[[-1,1],[0,1],[1,1]]` is *literally identical* to Entry 10 input\n- Entry 9 input `[[0,0],[0,1],[0,2]]` is *literally identical* to Entry 10 output\n\nThis violates the existing convention of named shape reuse.\n\n**Rule 4 (Fewest Elements)**: Adding two short constants is the minimal fix.\n\n## Refactoring\n\nI'll extract `BLINKER_V` and `BLINKER_H` following the existing convention, and update the top comment to document the new cycle relationship."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_cbc312844f284a20ab600041","name":"edit","arguments":{"edits":[{"newText":"// Named shapes make the relationships between patterns explicit: the\n// L-tromino survives reproduction (entries 2 & 3), the diamond is both a\n// still life and the result of plus-sign overpopulation (entries 5 & 6),\n// the block is a still life whose input equals its output (entry 8), and\n// the Blinker's vertical and horizontal forms cycle into each other\n